<Layout
  title="How to Choose a Pressure Transducer: Selection Guide"
  description="Step-by-step pressure transducer selection: pressure range, accuracy, output signal, process connection and wetted materials, with practical engineering rules."
>
  <BlogPostTemplate
    title="How to Choose a Pressure Transducer: Complete Selection Guide"
    category="Buying Guides"
    date="2026-08-29"
    readTime={15}
    author="PRESSUREPOINT Team"
    toc={toc}
    related={related}
  >
    <p>
      Choosing a pressure transducer is a decision with six independent variables: pressure range, accuracy, output signal, process connection, wetted materials and environmental rating. Get one wrong and the instrument either fails early, reads inaccurately or costs twice what it should. This guide walks through each decision in order, with the practical engineering rules that let a buyer specify a transducer correctly the first time — without needing a manufacturer's application engineer on call.
    </p>

    <h2 id="why-selection">Why Transducer Selection Matters</h2>
    <p>
      A pressure transducer converts pressure into a proportional electrical signal — typically 4-20 mA, 0-10 V or RS485. Every application, from a hydraulic press to a sterile food process line, puts a different combination of demands on that conversion. The transducer is usually the cheapest component in the loop and the easiest to replace, yet it is the source of the measurement the whole system trusts. The cost of a wrong selection is rarely the transducer itself; it is the downtime, the re-engineering and the missed process readings while the problem is diagnosed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The six decisions below form a logical sequence: range and accuracy define what you need to measure, output and connection define how the instrument talks to your controller, and environment plus wetted parts define whether it survives the installation. Work through them in order and most applications resolve to a single sensible model.
    </p>

    <h2 id="step-range">Step 1: Pressure Range & Overload</h2>
    <p>
      The pressure range is the first decision because it constrains everything else. Two rules of thumb govern it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Operate in the middle of the range</strong> — a transducer is most accurate and stable between roughly 25% and 75% of full scale. If your working pressure is 200 bar, a 0-400 bar unit gives better long-term stability than a 0-250 bar unit running near its ceiling.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep overload headroom</strong> — select a range whose <strong>overload rating</strong> covers your worst-case transient (water hammer, valve slamming, pump surge). Standard industrial transducers typically withstand 150-200% of full scale without damage; check the datasheet's overload figure, not just the calibrated range.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Working pressure</th>
            <th>Recommended calibrated range</th>
            <th>Typical overload (200%)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>0-10 bar</td>
            <td>0-16 bar</td>
            <td>32 bar</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>0-100 bar</td>
            <td>0-160 bar</td>
            <td>320 bar</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>0-250 bar</td>
            <td>0-400 bar</td>
            <td>800 bar</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>0-400 bar</td>
            <td>0-600 bar</td>
            <td>1200 bar</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Compound and vacuum ranges</strong> are separate products: a standard gauge transducer measures positive pressure only, while a compound unit measures both vacuum and positive pressure (for example -1 to 3 bar), and an absolute unit references a perfect vacuum rather than local atmospheric pressure. If your process pulls a vacuum at any point, state it explicitly — it changes the sensing element specification.
    </p>
    <p>
      For hydraulic and pneumatic systems running up to 600 bar, see the <a href="/products/industrial/">industrial pressure transducer range</a>, which covers vacuum through 600 bar with 150-200% overload as standard.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reference">Gauge vs Absolute vs Sealed Reference</h2>
    <p>
      Every pressure measurement is a difference between two pressures. The <strong>reference</strong> defines the second point, and it is a decision buyers make implicitly — often without realizing it changes the product. Three references dominate industrial practice:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gauge (relative) pressure</strong> — measures against ambient atmospheric pressure. The sensor has a vented reference chamber open to the atmosphere. This is the default for hydraulics, pneumatics, water systems and most process applications, because the value operators care about is the difference from the surrounding air.</li>
      <li><strong>Absolute pressure</strong> — measures against a sealed vacuum reference inside the sensor. Readings do not change with weather or altitude. Absolute transducers are required for barometric measurement, vacuum processes, altitude compensation and any application where the true pressure above zero must be known.</li>
      <li><strong>Sealed gauge</strong> — a gauge transducer with a sealed reference chamber calibrated at the factory to standard atmosphere (usually 1.013 bar). It behaves like gauge pressure but has no vent, which suits outdoor and humid installations where a vent tube could draw in moisture.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Reference</th>
            <th>Reference point</th>
            <th>Typical applications</th>
            <th>Atmospheric influence</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Gauge</td>
            <td>Ambient atmosphere (vented)</td>
            <td>Hydraulics, pumps, tanks, compressors</td>
            <td>Reading shifts with weather/altitude</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Absolute</td>
            <td>Sealed vacuum</td>
            <td>Barometric, vacuum, altitude, custody transfer</td>
            <td>None</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sealed gauge</td>
            <td>Factory-sealed atmosphere</td>
            <td>Outdoor, humid, mobile equipment</td>
            <td>Small factory-set offset</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The rule of thumb: if you need the pressure <em>relative to the environment</em>, choose gauge; if you need the pressure <em>relative to a vacuum</em>, choose absolute. When in doubt and the application is outdoor or condensation-prone, sealed gauge removes the vent-tube failure mode entirely.
    </p>

    <h2 id="step-accuracy">Step 2: Accuracy & Temperature Effects</h2>
    <p>
      Accuracy is quoted as a percentage of full scale (% FS) — for example ±0.25% FS on a 0-100 bar unit means a possible error of ±0.25 bar anywhere in the range. The practical accuracy tiers are:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Accuracy class</th>
            <th>Typical error (0-100 bar unit)</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>±0.5% FS</td>
            <td>±0.5 bar</td>
            <td>General monitoring, hydraulics, alarms</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>±0.25% FS</td>
            <td>±0.25 bar</td>
            <td>Process control, OEM machinery</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>±0.1% FS</td>
            <td>±0.1 bar</td>
            <td>Test stands, custody-grade measurement, calibration</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      What buyers often miss is that <strong>temperature drift can exceed the base accuracy</strong>. A ±0.1% FS transducer with poor temperature compensation can drift more in a 40 °C ambient swing than a ±0.25% unit with tight compensation. Check two datasheet figures: <strong>zero temperature coefficient</strong> and <strong>span temperature coefficient</strong>, usually expressed as % FS per 10 °C or per 50 °C. For outdoor, steam-adjacent or engine-bay installations, temperature behavior matters as much as the headline accuracy number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Non-linearity, hysteresis and repeatability are usually bundled into the accuracy figure, but they behave differently: hysteresis repeats itself, non-linearity is systematic, and repeatability is random. If your application repeatedly cycles through the same pressure (hydraulic presses, fatigue testing), prioritize a transducer with low hysteresis and high repeatability. The <a href="/products/high-accuracy/">high accuracy series</a> at ±0.1% FS with low temperature drift is built for precisely this kind of precision work.
    </p>

    <h2 id="step-output">Step 3: Output Signal</h2>
    <p>
      The output signal must match your controller's analog input. The three mainstream options:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>4-20 mA (two-wire)</strong> — the industrial default. Current signals are immune to cable resistance, run hundreds of meters, and the 4 mA live zero provides built-in fault detection. Virtually every PLC and DCS accepts it. See the <a href="/blog/pressure-transducer-4-20ma/">full 4-20 mA guide</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>0-10 V or 0-5 V (three-wire)</strong> — simple and convenient for short runs, local displays and OEM boards, but voltage drops over long cable and 0 V is ambiguous between zero pressure and a dead sensor.</li>
      <li><strong>RS485 / Modbus</strong> — digital output, multi-drop capability (up to 128 devices on one bus), direct engineering-unit values and remote configuration. Choose this for IoT, SCADA and multi-sensor panels.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Aspect</th>
            <th>4-20 mA</th>
            <th>0-10 V</th>
            <th>RS485 Modbus</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Wires</td>
            <td>2 (loop-powered)</td>
            <td>3 (supply + signal + common)</td>
            <td>2 data + supply</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Max practical distance</td>
            <td>Hundreds of meters</td>
            <td>Typically &lt;100 m</td>
            <td>1200 m per segment</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Fault detection</td>
            <td>0 / &lt;4 mA = fault</td>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>CRC + device status</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Multi-drop</td>
            <td>No (1 per loop)</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>Up to 128 devices</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Best for</td>
            <td>PLC/DCS, plants, remote</td>
            <td>HVAC, local meters, OEM</td>
            <td>IoT, SCADA, multi-sensor</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When in doubt, 4-20 mA is the safe default: it is the most universally supported signal in industrial automation, and it works with any controller that has a current input — which is nearly all of them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="step-connection">Step 4: Process Connection & Wetted Parts</h2>
    <p>
      The process connection physically marries the transducer to your system, and the wetted material determines chemical compatibility. This is the decision that most often gets re-engineered on site.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Common process connections</strong> — always state the thread and the sealing method together:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Connection</th>
            <th>Where it is common</th>
            <th>Sealing</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>G1/4" (BSPP)</td>
            <td>Europe, hydraulics, pneumatics</td>
            <td>Sealing ring / washer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1/4" NPT</td>
            <td>North America, general industry</td>
            <td>Tapered thread + sealant</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>M20×1.5</td>
            <td>Process industry, transmitters</td>
            <td>Gasket / O-ring</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1/2" NPT or G1/2"</td>
            <td>Higher-pressure hydraulic</td>
            <td>Tapered thread / washer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Flush / hygienic (DIN 11851, Tri-Clamp)</td>
            <td>Food, pharma, viscous media</td>
            <td>Clamp or threaded ferrule</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Wetted material</strong> is the second half of this decision. 316L stainless steel handles the large majority of industrial media — water, oils, hydraulic fluid, air, steam and most mild chemicals. Aggressive media need a different diaphragm:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hastelloy C-276</strong> — strong acids and chlorides; the standard answer for corrosive chemical duty.</li>
      <li><strong>Ceramic (Al₂O₃)</strong> — excellent chemical resistance and lower cost for water/wastewater and mild media, with high overload capability.</li>
      <li><strong>Flush diaphragm</strong> — no dead volume and no cavity for media to clog or crystallize; required for pastes, slurries and hygiene processes. See the <a href="/products/flush-diaphragm/">flush diaphragm range</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If your media is unknown, viscous, or contains solids, state that too — it points to a flush or diaphragm-sealed construction rather than a standard pressure port.
    </p>

    <h2 id="step-environment">Step 5: Environment, Hazardous Areas & Protection</h2>
    <p>
      The ambient environment decides the transducer's enclosure rating and any hazardous-area certification. Four checks:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ingress protection</strong> — IP65 handles indoor splash; IP67/IP68 is for washdown, outdoor and submersible duty. A <a href="/products/submersible/">submersible level transmitter</a>, for example, needs IP68 with a ventilation tube to reference atmospheric pressure.</li>
      <li><strong>Ambient temperature</strong> — standard transducers operate roughly -20 to +85 °C ambient. Steam lines, engines and desert installations may need an extended-temperature variant or remote mounting.</li>
      <li><strong>Hazardous areas</strong> — if the site is classified Zone 0/1/2 (gas) or Zone 20/21/22 (dust), the transducer must carry an ATEX or IECEx certificate for that zone. Explosion-proof transmitters with flameproof enclosures cover Zone 1/2 gas duty; see the <a href="/products/explosion-proof/">explosion-proof range</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Vibration and shock</strong> — mobile equipment, compressors and presses generate sustained vibration. Check the datasheet's vibration rating (e.g. 10 g, 20-2000 Hz) and avoid cantilever-mounted sensors on high-vibration machinery without a damping mount.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="step-electrical">Step 6: Electrical Connection</h2>
    <p>
      The electrical termination must match your cable and the installation environment. The common options:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Termination</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
            <th>Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>DIN 43650 (Hirschmann)</td>
            <td>Hydraulics, OEM, fast field replacement</td>
            <td>IP65 with mating connector, 2-3 pins</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>M12×1 circular</td>
            <td>Automation, robots, washdown</td>
            <td>IP67, pre-wired or field-attachable</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Flying lead / cable</td>
            <td>Permanent installations, submersible</td>
            <td>Length specified in metres; screen grounded one end</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Vent tube</td>
            <td>Sealed-gauge / submersible</td>
            <td>Terminates in a dry location, away from moisture</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      For sealed-gauge transducers in humid or condensing environments, a <strong>vented cable or connector with a desiccant filter</strong> prevents moisture from condensing inside the reference chamber — a common cause of drifting zero readings.
    </p>

    <h2 id="units">Pressure Units & Conversion</h2>
    <p>
      Pressure transducers are specified and scaled in several units depending on the region and industry: bar and mbar across Europe and much of Asia, psi in North America, MPa and kPa in many hydraulic and process applications, and mmHg or mH₂O in specialist fields such as medical and level measurement. The transducer's calibrated range is defined in one unit — every other unit is a conversion of that.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Unit</th>
            <th>Equivalent to</th>
            <th>Typical use</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>1 bar</td>
            <td>100 kPa = 14.5038 psi = 10 mH₂O</td>
            <td>Europe/Asia industrial, hydraulics</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1 MPa</td>
            <td>10 bar = 145.038 psi</td>
            <td>Hydraulic and heavy industry</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1 psi</td>
            <td>0.0689 bar = 6.8948 kPa</td>
            <td>North America, pneumatic systems</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1 kPa</td>
            <td>0.01 bar = 0.145 psi</td>
            <td>Low-range process and HVAC</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1 mH₂O</td>
            <td>0.098 bar = 9.80665 kPa</td>
            <td>Level measurement (fresh water)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The conversion mistake that causes the most field problems is mixing units between the transducer range and the controller scale: ordering a 0-10 bar transducer and scaling the PLC input for 0-100 psi reads 10× too low (10 bar ≈ 145 psi). State the unit explicitly in the RFQ, keep the same unit on the controller, and convert once — on paper — before commissioning.
    </p>

    <h2 id="calibration">Calibration, Certification & Documentation</h2>
    <p>
      Factory calibration and certification are part of the specification, not an afterthought. Three items matter to a buyer:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Calibration certificate</strong> — a per-unit calibration report lists the measured output at defined pressure points (typically 0, 25, 50, 75 and 100% of span), the as-found and as-left errors, and the standards used. NIST-traceable or ISO 17025 calibration is worth asking for when the measurement feeds a quality record or a customer deliverable.</li>
      <li><strong>CE marking</strong> — required for sale in the EU and EEA. For pressure transducers this covers EMC (electromagnetic compatibility) and, where applicable, the Pressure Equipment Directive and ATEX for hazardous areas. A CE declaration of conformity should be available on request.</li>
      <li><strong>Material certificates</strong> — a material certificate (EN 10204 3.1) confirms the 316L or Hastelloy wetted parts meet the specified grade. Oil &amp; gas, food and pharmaceutical buyers routinely require it for traceability.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For ongoing accuracy, plan a <strong>recalibration interval</strong>. General industrial duty commonly uses 12 months; higher-accuracy applications or those feeding quality records may shorten it to 6 months. A transducer that drifts out of spec between calibrations is usually caught at the next certificate — which is exactly why documented calibration matters. Our <a href="/services/calibration/">calibration service</a> covers factory multi-point calibration and recertification with per-serial-number certificates.
    </p>

