    <h2 id="rfq">RFQ Checklist: What to Send a Manufacturer</h2>
    <p>
      When you are ready to request a quotation, a complete specification gets you a firm price and a confirmed model in one round — an incomplete one costs days of back-and-forth. Send these ten items and the supplier can quote immediately:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Pressure range</strong> — the calibrated range you want (for example 0-16 bar), not just the working pressure.</li>
      <li><strong>Media</strong> — what the diaphragm touches (hydraulic oil, water, steam, acid, gas). Include temperature and whether it is viscous or has solids.</li>
      <li><strong>Output signal</strong> — 4-20 mA, 0-10 V, RS485, or a custom value. State the supply voltage your system provides.</li>
      <li><strong>Accuracy required</strong> — ±0.25% FS or ±0.1% FS; be explicit if the value feeds a control or safety function.</li>
      <li><strong>Process connection</strong> — thread and sealing (G1/4 BSPP with washer, 1/4 NPT with sealant, M20×1.5, flush or hygienic).</li>
      <li><strong>Wetted material</strong> — 316L, Hastelloy, ceramic, or leave it to the supplier with your media listed.</li>
      <li><strong>Electrical termination</strong> — DIN 43650, M12, flying lead with length, or cable with vent tube.</li>
      <li><strong>Ambient conditions</strong> — operating temperature range, IP rating needed, vibration levels, hazardous-area zone if classified.</li>
      <li><strong>Quantity and timeline</strong> — annual volume and target lead time; this determines whether you qualify for OEM pricing.</li>
      <li><strong>Certification needs</strong> — CE, ATEX/IECEx, NIST-traceable calibration certificate, material certificates, or test reports.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Send your specification through the <a href="/contact/">inquiry form</a> and PRESSUREPOINT engineers will confirm a model, datasheet and price — usually within one working day.
    </p>

    <h2 id="mistakes">Common Selection Mistakes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Range too tight</strong> — sizing the range to the normal working pressure instead of the worst-case transient leaves no overload headroom; a single surge damages the sensing element.</li>
      <li><strong>Confusing accuracy with temperature performance</strong> — comparing headline % FS figures while ignoring temperature coefficients produces surprising drift in real installations.</li>
      <li><strong>Signal mismatch</strong> — ordering a 0-10 V transducer for a PLC with current inputs only, or vice versa, is the most common re-order reason. Confirm the controller input before ordering.</li>
      <li><strong>Thread mismatch</strong> — G1/4 and 1/4 NPT look similar and are not interchangeable; wrong threads cause leaks and stripped ports.</li>
      <li><strong>Ignoring media compatibility</strong> — 316L is not universal; chlorides, strong acids and viscous media each demand a different diaphragm material or construction.</li>
      <li><strong>Skipping the hazardous-area check</strong> — installing an uncertified transducer in a classified zone is both a safety violation and an insurance problem.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <h3>What accuracy do I actually need for a hydraulic system?</h3>
    <p>
      ±0.25% FS is the usual standard for hydraulic and pneumatic monitoring. If the measurement feeds a safety function, a control loop that affects product quality, or a test where the value is reported, step up to ±0.1% FS. For simple alarm or display duty, ±0.5% FS is often sufficient.
    </p>
    <h3>Should I choose 4-20 mA or 0-10 V?</h3>
    <p>
      Choose 4-20 mA unless your controller has only voltage inputs and the cable run is short. Current loops are immune to cable resistance, support longer runs, and give you free fault detection via the 4 mA live zero.
    </p>
    <h3>What pressure range should I pick if my system peaks at 150 bar?</h3>
    <p>
      A 0-250 bar transducer is the typical choice: it keeps normal operation below 75% of span and its overload rating (typically 200%, i.e. 500 bar) covers transients. Avoid a 0-160 bar unit if surges are common — the overload margin is thinner.
    </p>
    <h3>Can one transducer measure both vacuum and positive pressure?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes — a compound range (for example -1 to 3 bar) measures both vacuum and positive pressure. A standard gauge transducer measures positive pressure only. Specify the compound option explicitly at ordering.
    </p>
    <h3>How do I know if I need an explosion-proof transducer?</h3>
    <p>
      If the installation area is a classified hazardous zone (for example Zone 1 or Zone 2 for gases), the transducer must carry an appropriate ATEX or IECEx certification. Check the site's hazardous-area classification drawing; when in doubt, ask the plant safety engineer before specifying.
    </p>
    <h3>What is the difference between gauge and absolute pressure?</h3>
    <p>
      Gauge pressure measures against ambient atmospheric pressure (vented reference) and is the default for hydraulics, pneumatics and tanks. Absolute pressure measures against a sealed vacuum reference and is required for barometric, vacuum and altitude applications. Sealed gauge is a third option for outdoor or humid duty where a vent could draw in moisture.
    </p>
    <h3>Does a higher-accuracy transducer need recalibration more often?</h3>
    <p>
      Not necessarily — recalibration interval is driven by the application's accuracy requirement, not the instrument's class. A ±0.1% FS unit feeding a quality record may be recalibrated every 6 months; a ±0.5% FS alarm sensor might run for years. Follow the datasheet's long-term stability figure and your quality system's schedule.
    </p>

    <h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
    <p>
      Correct transducer selection is a sequence, not a leap: fix the range and accuracy, match the output to your controller, then verify connection, wetted parts, environment and electrical termination against the datasheet. Working through the six steps above resolves the overwhelming majority of applications to one model — and eliminates most of the reasons transducers get returned or re-specified. It also shortens the RFQ cycle: a complete specification gets you a firm quote on the first pass, and an instrument that arrives already matched to your installation.
    </p>
